 \label{lem:energy-optimization}
Let $X$ be a finite set and $\mathscr{P}$ be a set of partitions of $X$, which contains the discrete partition $\mathcal{D} := \{\{x\} : x \in X\}$, the trivial partition $\mathcal{T} := \{X\}$, and which is closed under taking common refinements (i.e., if $\mathcal{P}, \mathcal{Q} \in \mathscr{P}$, then $\mathcal{P} \vee \mathcal{Q} \in \mathscr{P}$). Consider two functions $\mathcal{E}, \mathcal{C} : \mathscr{P} \to \mathbf{R}$ with the following properties:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[$(i)$] \emph{(Energy is nondecreasing)}. For any $\mathcal{P}, \mathcal{P}' \in \mathscr{P}$ such that $\mathcal{P}'$ is finer than $\mathcal{P}$, one has $\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{P}) \le \mathcal{E}(\mathcal{P}')$.
    \item[$(ii)$] \emph{(Complexity is subadditive)}. For any $\mathcal{P}, \mathcal{Q} \in \mathscr{P}$, one has $\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{P} \vee \mathcal{Q}) \le \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{P}) + \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{Q})$. Moreover, $\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{T}) = 0$.
\end{itemize}
Let $\varepsilon > 0$. Then there exists a partition $\mathcal{P} \in \mathscr{P}$ with $\mathcal{C}(\mathcal{P}) \le \frac{1}{\varepsilon}$, such that for any $\mathcal{Q} \in \mathscr{P}$, one has
\[
    \mathcal{E}(\mathcal{P} \vee \mathcal{Q}) - \mathcal{E}(\mathcal{P}) \le \varepsilon\ \mathcal{C}(\mathcal{Q}) \left(\mathcal{E}(\mathcal{D}) - \mathcal{E}(\mathcal{T})\right).
\]

